[Music]  as an offensive lineman you're always  working behind the scenes you know how  to you know handle success  humbly  because when you have that success your  your name's not going to be printed in a  newspaper article maybe you know you get  a shout out you know the o-line as a  unit would get a shout out from the  running backs quarterbacks coaches  whatever but we knew to never get too  high after success never get too low you  know if there's any kind of failure that  it just really took you know five guys  to you know grind day in and day out and  look to keep getting better and build as  a unit  my name is jared veldir i grew up in  grand rapids michigan uh born and raised  football even professional football you  know i didn't really see that in my  future much like maybe college football  but i like basketball a lot more coach  otterbein came and watched one of my  basketball games  and then offered me a scholarship to  play football after and  uh i accepted pretty quick because i was  already familiar with the school my  brother went there played basketball  there i i could see uh you know the  value in being at an institution like  that for four plus years  the nfl thought first popped in my head  after my  freshman year i was able to put on some  good size strength and speed and a  couple guys just started joking about it  like hey you're just as big as these  guys that are you know getting drafted  or you know in the combine and that are  playing in the nfl and it never seemed  too stressful and it just seemed kind of  you know a lighthearted fun goal to go  about you we finished our our football  season in november and then immediately  i signed with an agent and that whole  process started to go um you know heard  my name called with the 69th pick in the  in the in the draft  which was the third round  just uh kind of a whirlwind you know  just instant like elation and then just  craziness  looking back at the transition between  division two and the nfl getting moved  from left tackle the right tackle  probably sticks out is the biggest  challenge  because i played left tackle my whole  time at hillsdale and played left tackle  for the first seven years of the nfl  career and when i took those initial  right tackle sets and it just felt so  awkward you know my body was just so  programmed to move from from a  left-handed stance i mean i  wanted to  [Music]  basically quit in training camp because  i felt like i was just so worthless and  that i was you know no longer that one  of those five guys that was accountable  to one another and that i couldn't do my  job and i didn't want to do that to the  group and the team and it's never fun  when you have to learn through playing  in front of you know 60 000 plus people  but uh  again just kind of keep your head down  and try to get better and correct things  and fix it as you go and that's what i  did and figured it out and so it's nice  being able to  take some of those lessons and now uh  you know try to apply them outside of  football you know trying to to raise a  young family and you know now being back  in west michigan and you know just  life-changing sometimes you feel like  you can maybe lose your footing but with  what i've you know learned i'm able to  kind of stay grounded if that makes  sense  you know something that everyone who's  gone to hillsdale's heard you know a  million times it's the people  and i think it really applies because uh  you know without that great group of  people there i don't know how many  people would stick around and survive  and thrive and have a really you know  good academic career athletic career  whatever it is there it's a it's a tough  place so it was a losing record my  freshman year and then uh it seemed like  each year i think it was each year we we  had gotten better improved uh and then  my senior year we had made it to the  division two playoffs for the first time  in school history and coach otterbein  was a great coach  you know i  really you know tipped my hat to him  being able to work with  18 to you know 22 year old  kids and turn him into men and i think  that was  always his number one goal and you when  you're going through you know the same  kind of challenges the same difficulties  the same you know stages of life and uh  you know you're doing it together you  know non-stop uh you need to forge some  really special relationships and was  definitely able to do that at hillsdale  not trying to put words in your mouth  coach otter but  you know if you can mold those those  good men those good human beings that  the winds would follow that because that  was the most important piece without  that then the winds wouldn't happen

